### gRPC API

The contract is `api/proto/user/v1/user.proto`; `make gen` writes the Go stubs to `api/gen/user/v1` (commit them, like the other generated code). The server listens on `grpc.port` (default `9090`).

| RPC                                   | Auth   | Description                                                        |
| ------------------------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `user.v1.UserService/CreateUser`      | public | Register a new user                                                |
| `user.v1.UserService/Login`           | public | Exchange email/password for a bearer token                         |
| `user.v1.UserService/ListPublicUsers` | public | Public user directory (names only, cursor)                         |
| `user.v1.UserService/GetUser`         | bearer | Get user by ID                                                     |
| `user.v1.UserService/UpdateUser`      | bearer | Update a user (own account only)                                   |
| `user.v1.UserService/DeleteUser`      | bearer | Delete a user (own account only)                                   |
| `user.v1.UserService/ListUsers`       | bearer | List users (admin, page-paginated)                                 |
| `grpc.health.v1.Health/Check`         | public | SERVING while the process runs, NOT_SERVING once a shutdown starts |

Metadata keys mirror the HTTP headers: `authorization: Bearer <accessToken>` (the token from `Login`), `accept-language` for the error language, `x-request-id` for correlation (echoed back). Errors come back as gRPC status codes with the same localized messages the HTTP API sends (`InvalidArgument`, `Unauthenticated`, `PermissionDenied`, `NotFound`, `AlreadyExists`, …).

Server reflection is on outside production (`grpc.reflection`), so [grpcurl](https://github.com/fullstorydev/grpcurl) needs no proto files:

```bash
grpcurl -plaintext localhost:9090 list
grpcurl -plaintext -d '{"email":"a@b.co","name":"Ada","password":"hunter2-pass"}' localhost:9090 user.v1.UserService/CreateUser
grpcurl -plaintext localhost:9090 grpc.health.v1.Health/Check
```

Auth is deny-by-default: every RPC needs a token unless `UserHandler.Public()` lists it, and `Throttled()` names the credential RPCs limited per peer (`grpc.login_rate_limit`). Every call also runs under `grpc.request_timeout`. To add an RPC: edit the proto, run `make gen`, implement the method on `transport/grpc.UserHandler`, and add it to `Public()` only if it must work without a token.
{{- end}}

{{- if .HasWorker}}

### Worker process

This binary subscribes to a message broker ({{.MessageQueue}}) and processes
events emitted by an upstream publisher (e.g. a sibling project generated
with `nova new --transport=http --queue={{.MessageQueue}}`). The current
handler set:

| Topic            | Handler                                        | Side effect                                        |
| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| `user.created`   | `transport/worker/v1/user.Handler.Handle`      | Inserts a row into `user_audit_log`, once per `eventId` |

Every message gets `worker.max_attempts` tries, `worker.retry_backoff` apart (doubling), each under `worker.handler_timeout`; after that it is parked with the reason on {{if eq .MessageQueue "kafka"}}`<topic>.dlq`{{else}}`<queue>.dlq` (via the `<queue>.dlx` exchange){{end}} and the stream moves on. Return `worker.Poison(err)` from a handler for input that can never succeed so it is parked at once; return a plain error for anything transient. A consumer that loses its subscription exits non-zero so the orchestrator restarts it.

End-to-end demo, with a publisher project generated next to this one:

1. Here: `cp .env.example .env`, `docker compose up -d {{if eq .Database "mysql"}}mysql{{else}}postgres{{end}} {{.MessageQueue}}`, then `make migrate-up`, which creates `users` and `user_audit_log`.
2. Generate the publisher in a sibling directory:
   `nova new {{.ProjectName}}-api --transport=http --database={{.Database}} --queue={{.MessageQueue}} --cache=none`.
3. There: `cp .env.example .env`, add a JWT keypair (its README shows how), and set `DB_NAME`, `DB_USER` and `DB_PASSWORD` to `{{.ProjectName}}` so it writes to this project's database, whose migrations already created `users`. {{if eq .MessageQueue "rabbitmq"}}Both projects default to the `events` exchange, so the broker settings already match.{{else}}Both projects use the `user.created` topic on `localhost:9092`, so the broker settings already match.{{end}}
4. Run the worker here (`go run main.go worker`), then the publisher there (`go run main.go api`).{{if eq .MessageQueue "rabbitmq"}} Start the worker first: it declares and binds the queue, and RabbitMQ drops a message published before any queue is bound.{{else}} Kafka keeps the topic, and a new consumer group starts from the oldest offset, so the order does not matter.{{end}}
5. `curl -X POST localhost:8080/api/v1/users -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"email":"ada@example.com","name":"Ada","password":"hunter2-pass"}'`.
6. `SELECT * FROM user_audit_log;` shows the inbound `user.created` row. Redeliver the same message and the second insert is ignored (`audit: duplicate delivery ignored` in the log).
{{- end}}

